outputs.with:
**Finding (Correctness — Required):** Indexing `items[0]` assumes at least one element exists. On an empty array, `items[0]` is `undefined`, causing `.name` to throw a runtime `TypeError` and leaving callers with an implicit unsafe type boundary.

**Scope & Checks:** Inspected the single PR line statically; no local test runner or typechecker was run.

**Correction:**
```ts
const first = items.length > 0 ? items[0].name : null;
```

**Regression Example:**
```ts
import assert from "node:assert/strict";

function firstItemName(items: Array<{ name: string }>): string | null {
  const first = items.length > 0 ? items[0].name : null;
  return first;
}

assert.equal(firstItemName([]), null);
assert.equal(firstItemName([{ name: "widget" }]), "widget");
```
